# CEA, CUA, CBA - and why cost-of-illness answers a different question entirely
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical","b":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical"}
next: {"title":"What is a life worth in money, and who decided that?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/what-is-a-life-worth-in-money-and-who.cast"}

## Same Drug, Four Questions
voice: male
level: basic

    text q_top "Which should the health system buy?" x 500 y 655 font_size 40 hidden true
    text n_head "Nusinersen for SMA" #b5482e x 250 y 555 font_size 36 hidden true
    text n_c "ΔC ≈ 5 000 000 kr" x 250 y 480 font_size 32 hidden true
    text n_e "ΔE ≈ 1.5 QALYs" x 250 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s_head "Statins for prevention" #2f6b8f x 690 y 555 font_size 36 hidden true
    text s_c "ΔC ≈ 1 500 kr" x 690 y 480 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s_e "ΔE ≈ 0.05 QALYs" x 690 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    box decision "Nusinersen or statins?" x 500 y 500 width 360 height 76 font_size 30 hidden true
    box q_cea "CEA: cost per natural unit" x 735 y 630 width 440 height 76 font_size 28 hidden true
    box q_cua "CUA: cost per QALY" x 735 y 360 width 440 height 76 font_size 28 hidden true
    box q_cba "CBA: benefit minus cost, in kroner" x 265 y 360 width 440 height 76 font_size 28 hidden true
    box q_coi "COI: what the illness costs us" #b5482e x 265 y 630 width 440 height 76 font_size 28 hidden true
    edge e_cea decision -> q_cea hidden true
    edge e_cua decision -> q_cua hidden true
    edge e_cba decision -> q_cba hidden true
    edge e_coi decision -> q_coi #b5482e hidden true
    text lab_cea "milestones vs heart attacks" x 735 y 692 font_size 26 hidden true
    text lab_cua "3.3 mill. vs 30 000 kr per QALY" x 735 y 295 font_size 26 hidden true

This series is about four different ways of asking whether a health technology is worth buying — pick the wrong one and the numbers you produce cannot answer the question you were asked.
    draw q_top

Take nusinersen for spinal muscular atrophy: with illustrative numbers, about five million kroner extra per patient, buying roughly one and a half quality-adjusted life years.
    draw n_head n_c n_e

    pause 0.3

And the unglamorous comparison: statins in primary prevention, some fifteen hundred kroner per patient, buying about five hundredths of a QALY.
    draw s_head s_c s_e

Both are real interventions with real effects, and so far these four numbers tell you nothing about which one the system should buy.
    highlight n_c n_e s_c s_e effect glow

    pause 0.5

Because the answer depends entirely on which question you put to them.
    clear

Here is the choice — and here are the four questions an economist can ask about it.
    draw decision

First, cost-effectiveness analysis proper: cost per natural unit of health — kroner per motor milestone gained, kroner per heart attack avoided.
    draw e_cea q_cea lab_cea

It needs only an outcome you can count, which makes it transparent and hard to argue with — but milestones and heart attacks share no scale, so it can never rank these two against each other.
    focus q_cea lab_cea

Second, cost-utility analysis, which converts every effect into QALYs: three point three million kroner per QALY for nusinersen, about thirty thousand for the statin.
    draw e_cua q_cua lab_cua

On that single scale the boring intervention buys over a hundred times more health per krone — the comparison a health system actually needs.
    highlight lab_cua effect glow

Its strength is exactly that: everything becomes comparable. Its weakness is that it assumes a QALY counts the same whoever receives it — which is why Norway bolts alvorlighet, severity, on top of it.
    point at.ref q_cua gesture circle

    pause 0.4

Third, cost-benefit analysis, which puts the health gain itself into kroner, so health can be weighed against roads and schools — at the price of having to name what a life year is worth in money.
    draw e_cba q_cba

And fourth, the odd one out: a cost-of-illness study, adding up what spinal muscular atrophy costs Norway each year in treatment, care and lost work.
    draw e_coi q_coi

There is no alternative inside it, no comparison, no decision — it measures a burden, and it can rank nothing at all.
    mark mark_coi target q_coi kind cross
    delivery grave

Yet it is the figure that reaches ministries and newspapers, quoted as if a large burden by itself proved a treatment worth funding.

Four questions, four answers — and only this one can rank a rare drug against a common one. The method you pick decides what you are even allowed to conclude.
    mark mark_cua target q_cua kind box

## CEA, CUA, CBA side by side
voice: male
level: basic

    text title "CEA, CUA, CBA — side by side" x 500 y 712 font_size 30 hidden true
    path vline1 #8f887c points [[270,660],[270,190]] style.stroke_width 1.4 hidden true
    path vline2 #8f887c points [[505,660],[505,190]] style.stroke_width 1.4 hidden true
    path vline3 #8f887c points [[740,660],[740,190]] style.stroke_width 1.4 hidden true
    path hhead #8f887c points [[35,600],[975,600]] style.stroke_width 1.4 hidden true
    path h1 #8f887c points [[35,512],[975,512]] style.stroke_width 1.4 hidden true
    path h2 #8f887c points [[35,412],[975,412]] style.stroke_width 1.4 hidden true
    path h3 #8f887c points [[35,312],[975,312]] style.stroke_width 1.4 hidden true
    text h_cea "CEA" #2f6b8f x 387 y 640 font_size 30 hidden true
    text h_cua "CUA" #b5482e x 622 y 640 font_size 30 hidden true
    text h_cba "CBA" #8a5fa8 x 857 y 640 font_size 30 hidden true
    text rl1 "What it compares" x 150 y 556 font_size 23 hidden true
    text rl2 "What you must measure" x 150 y 462 font_size 23 hidden true
    text rl3 "Decision it supports" x 150 y 362 font_size 23 hidden true
    text rl4 "The nusinersen case" x 150 y 258 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c1_cea1 "Two options," #2f6b8f x 387 y 575 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c1_cea2 "one clinical outcome" #2f6b8f x 387 y 539 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c1_cua1 "Two options," #b5482e x 622 y 575 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c1_cua2 "health as a whole" #b5482e x 622 y 539 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c1_cba1 "Any two uses" #8a5fa8 x 857 y 575 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c1_cba2 "of the same money" #8a5fa8 x 857 y 539 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c2_cea1 "A natural unit" #2f6b8f x 387 y 481 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c2_cea2 "you can count" #2f6b8f x 387 y 445 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c2_cua1 "Utilities u, then" #b5482e x 622 y 481 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c2_cua2 "QALYs = sum of u·time" #b5482e x 622 y 445 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c2_cba1 "Every effect priced" #8a5fa8 x 857 y 481 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c2_cba2 "in kroner" #8a5fa8 x 857 y 445 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c3_cea1 "Best buy within" #2f6b8f x 387 y 381 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c3_cea2 "one disease area" #2f6b8f x 387 y 345 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c3_cua1 "Any health gain" #b5482e x 622 y 381 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c3_cua2 "vs the threshold" #b5482e x 622 y 345 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c3_cba1 "Worth doing at all?" #8a5fa8 x 857 y 381 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c3_cba2 "Net benefit > 0" #8a5fa8 x 857 y 345 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c4_cea1 "kr per motor" #2f6b8f x 387 y 278 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c4_cea2 "milestone gained" #2f6b8f x 387 y 242 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c4_cua1 "≈ 7.5 mill. kr" #b5482e x 622 y 278 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c4_cua2 "per QALY" #b5482e x 622 y 242 font_size 23 hidden true
    mark mark_icer target c4_cua1 kind box hidden true
    text c4_cba1 "Is that gain worth" #8a5fa8 x 857 y 278 font_size 23 hidden true
    text c4_cba2 "its price in kroner?" #8a5fa8 x 857 y 242 font_size 23 hidden true

One drug, four different questions — and the practical problem is which of those answers a committee can actually act on.
    draw title

    draw vline1 vline2 vline3 hhead h1 h2 h3 parallel true

Cost-effectiveness, cost-utility, cost-benefit — the arithmetic is identical in all three; only the unit in the denominator changes.
    draw h_cea h_cua h_cba parallel true

    pause 0.3

Row one: what is compared. Cost-effectiveness sets two options against a single clinical outcome — infections avoided, cases detected, motor milestones reached.
    draw rl1 c1_cea1 c1_cea2

Cost-utility takes the same two options but scores health as a whole: years of life weighted by the utility of living them.
    draw c1_cua1 c1_cua2

Cost-benefit compares any two uses of the same money — a drug against a road, if you like.
    draw c1_cba1 c1_cba2

    pause 0.4

Each buys that reach with a measurement: something you can simply count, utilities that build into QALYs, or a price in kroner for every single consequence.
    draw rl2 c2_cea1 c2_cea2 c2_cua1 c2_cua2 c2_cba1 c2_cba2

So each supports a different decision: the best buy inside one disease, any health gain against any other, or whether the thing is worth doing at all.
    draw rl3 c3_cea1 c3_cea2 c3_cua1 c3_cua2 c3_cba1 c3_cba2

    pause 0.5

Take nusinersen. In the CEA column the answer is kroner per motor milestone gained — clinically precise, and useless the moment you leave spinal muscular atrophy.
    draw rl4 c4_cea1 c4_cea2

In the CUA column it collapses to one number — illustratively, something like seven and a half million kroner per QALY.
    draw c4_cua1 c4_cua2

And now the contrast case can stand beside it: a hip replacement buys a QALY for roughly sixty thousand kroner.
    text cmp1 "Hip replacement: ≈ kr 60 000 per QALY" #d0865f x 500 y 150 font_size 26
    text cmp2 "same unit, same page, same decision" #d0865f x 500 y 115 font_size 22

Only this column lets those two numbers sit on the same page — over a hundred times more health per krone, bought by the boring operation nobody writes about.
    draw mark_icer delivery grave

That comparability is CUA's real strength — and its blind spot: it is deaf to anything that is not health, to dignity, to waiting time, to the school the same krone could have built.
    focus h_cua c1_cua1 c1_cua2 c2_cua1 c2_cua2 c3_cua1 c3_cua2 c4_cua1 c4_cua2 mark_icer

Cost-benefit hears all of that, and pays for it by having to put a money value on the health gain itself — which is exactly where its legitimacy gets thin, and where the next lecture starts.
    draw c4_cba1 c4_cba2

    pause 0.4

    clear keep ["title"]

One line each: CEA finds the best buy inside one problem, CUA puts every health gain in one unit, CBA puts health up against everything else.
    text k_cea "CEA — the best buy inside one problem" #2f6b8f x 500 y 520 font_size 32
    text k_cua "CUA — every health gain in one unit" #b5482e x 500 y 440 font_size 32
    text k_cba "CBA — health against everything else" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 360 font_size 32
    parallel true

Each step widens what you can compare — and raises what you must be willing to value.
    text k_syn "Widening what you can compare raises what you must value." x 500 y 250 font_size 28
    mark mark_syn target k_syn kind box
    delivery grave

## Cost-minimisation: the hidden assumption
voice: male
level: basic
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: One saving, three possible effects
    wtp_threshold: 275000
    points:
      - {label: CMA assumes ΔE = 0, effect: 0, cost: -20000, emphasize: true}
      - {label: ΔE = −0.15 QALY, effect: -0.15, cost: -20000}
      - {label: ΔE = +0.19 QALY, effect: 0.19, cost: -20000}
sources:
    - id: briggs2001
      title: The death of cost-minimization analysis? Health Economics 10(2):179–184
      authors: Briggs AH, O'Brien BJ
      year: 2001
      finding: Cost-minimisation is only justified when equivalence of effects is established by design, not inferred from a non-significant difference; the joint uncertainty in costs and effects should be analysed instead.

Beside CEA, CUA and CBA sits a fourth label on the list — the one that claims to need no effect measure at all.
    draw x_axis y_axis

    draw q_ne q_nw q_se q_sw parallel true

Our new formulation lands here: twenty thousand kroner cheaper per patient, and — by assumption — exactly no difference in health.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

Sitting precisely on the vertical axis is a very strong claim: with ΔE zero there is no ratio to compute and nothing to weigh, so you simply buy the cheaper one.
    highlight pt_0 effect glow

    pause 0.5

But that zero came from a trial, and its confidence interval ran from a loss of 0.15 QALYs to a gain of 0.19 — every point along this stretch fits the data equally well.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1 pt_2 pt_label_2

A p-value of 0.62 says we failed to detect a difference; it does not say we established there is none — the study was simply too small to tell a seventh of a life-year from zero.
    focus pt_1 pt_2 pt_label_1 pt_label_2

Bring in the threshold, 275 000 kroner per quality-adjusted life year, and that stretch stops being abstract.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

At the lower limit the point sits above the threshold line: we would be surrendering health worth more than the money we saved.
    highlight pt_1 effect glow

    clear

Net monetary benefit makes the same comparison in kroner: the health difference valued at the threshold, minus the extra cost.
    text nmb_title "NMB = λ·ΔE − ΔC,   λ = 275 000 kr per QALY" x 500 y 625 font_size 30

If the effects truly are identical, the new formulation is worth twenty thousand kroner per patient — and cost-minimisation's answer is the right one.
    text nmb_a "ΔE = 0:      0 + 20 000  =  +20 000 kr" x 500 y 545 font_size 30

At the bottom of the interval, the health given up is worth forty-one thousand, so the saving no longer covers it and net benefit turns negative.
    text nmb_b "ΔE = −0.15:   −41 250 + 20 000  =  −21 250 kr" #b5482e x 500 y 465 font_size 30

At the top it is cheaper and better — seventy-two thousand kroner of net benefit, a clear yes.
    text nmb_c "ΔE = +0.19:   +52 250 + 20 000  =  +72 250 kr" #2f6b8f x 500 y 385 font_size 30

One trial, and it supports a confident yes and a confident no; cost-minimisation reports the middle number and calls the matter settled.
    text verdict "The sign of the answer is unknown — and CMA never asks" x 500 y 290 font_size 28
    mark verdict_box target verdict kind box
    delivery grave

It is not always wrong — when equivalence is built into the design, a biosimilar with a formal equivalence trial, or the same infusion given at home instead of in hospital, comparing resource use alone is honest and quick.
    text caveat "Equivalence must be designed in, never inferred from p > 0.05" #8f887c cites ["briggs2001"] x 500 y 215 font_size 25

Applied to a non-significant result, though, it asserts an equivalence nobody ever tested — which is why Briggs and O'Brien asked, in a 2001 paper, whether cost-minimisation analysis was dead.

So run the full analysis and show the uncertainty: a zero you assumed is not a zero you measured.

## Cost-of-Illness Answers Nothing
voice: male
level: basic

Cost-minimisation hides an assumption; a cost-of-illness study counts something else entirely: what spinal muscular atrophy costs Norway in a year, starting with hospital care and the drugs themselves — four hundred million kroner.
    box direct "Hospital & drugs: kr 400 m" x 185 y 640 width 290 height 74 font_size 25

Then the production lost by patients and by parents who cut their hours, and the unpaid care given at home: a hundred and fifty million, and two hundred and fifty.
    box prod "Lost work: kr 150 m" x 500 y 640 width 250 height 74 font_size 25
    box care "Care at home: kr 250 m" x 815 y 640 width 290 height 74 font_size 25
    parallel true

    pause 0.3

Add them up and you have the headline — eight hundred million kroner a year; my figures are illustrative, but the shape of this study is exactly real.
    box burden "SMA burden: kr 800 m per year" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 480 width 460 height 74 font_size 30
    arrow e_direct direct -> burden
    arrow e_prod prod -> burden
    arrow e_care care -> burden

    pause 0.5

And here is the question this figure cannot answer: compared with what?
    text q_label "Compared with what?" #b5482e x 500 y 400 font_size 28

There is no second world here — no alternative Norway, doing something else — so nothing on this canvas is a difference, and without a difference there is no ΔC, no ΔE, and nothing to divide.
    focus burden delivery grave

Set the burden aside and look at the shape every economic evaluation actually has.
    clear

One world: a child with spinal muscular atrophy on current care, with cost C-zero and health E-zero.
    box care0 "Current care (0): C0, E0" x 500 y 270 width 400 height 74 font_size 30

A second world: the same child on nusinersen, with cost C-one and health E-one.
    box drug1 "Nusinersen (1): C1, E1" x 500 y 560 width 400 height 74 font_size 30

    pause 0.3

Subtract on the cost side, and the extra spending appears — roughly twenty million kroner more over that child's lifetime.
    arrow a_dc care0 -> drug1 curved #b5482e
    text lbl_dc "ΔC = kr 20 million" #b5482e x 720 y 415 font_size 28

Subtract on the health side, and say four extra quality-adjusted life years appear.
    arrow a_de care0 -> drug1 #2f6b8f
    text lbl_de "ΔE = 4 QALYs" #2f6b8f x 290 y 415 font_size 28

Divide one by the other and you get an ICER, around five million kroner per QALY — a number Beslutningsforum can argue about, because it is a comparison.
    text lbl_icer "ICER = ΔC / ΔE ≈ kr 5 m per QALY" x 500 y 680 font_size 30
    mark ann_icer target lbl_icer kind box

So why does that eight-hundred-million figure keep turning up in arguments about whether to buy the drug?
    clear

Because a big number persuades, and burden studies are usually commissioned by exactly those who want attention for the disease.
    show burden

They do one job genuinely well: they show where money and carers' hours already go, which helps plan services and reveals a condition nobody has been counting.
    text lbl_use "Shows where money already goes" #87a878 x 500 y 400 font_size 27

But the totals inflate easily — value a parent's lost wages here, then the same hours again as unpaid care, and you have counted them twice.
    show prod care e_prod e_care

And a large burden is not an opportunity: a disease can cost a fortune and still have nothing effective available to buy.
    text lbl_misuse "Big burden ≠ big opportunity" #b5482e x 500 y 335 font_size 27

    pause 0.4

Cost-of-illness measures the size of a problem; only a comparison of two worlds can tell you what to do about it.
    text lbl_verdict "Describes a problem. Ranks nothing." x 500 y 245 font_size 30
    mark ann_verdict target lbl_verdict kind box
    delivery grave

## Picking the right tool
voice: male
level: basic
use: decision_tree
with:
    root:
      id: q
      type: decision
      label: What must compete?
      children: [{label: One fixed goal, node: {id: cea, type: terminal, label: Cost per case detected}}, {label: All options compete, node: {id: cua, type: terminal, label: Cost per QALY}}, {label: Benefit leaves health, node: {id: cba, type: terminal, label: Costs and benefits in kroner}}, {label: Nothing compared, node: {id: coi, type: terminal, label: Cost of illness}}]

A cost-of-illness study names a burden without ever comparing two options — and the method you should have used was settled long before the costing began — at this square.
    draw node_q label_q

    pause 0.3

First branch: the objective is fixed and single — find latent tuberculosis among new arrivals — and every option you weigh produces that one thing and nothing else.
    draw edge_q_cea branchlabel_q_cea node_cea label_cea

Then the honest denominator is the natural unit: kr 12 000 per case detected by one strategy against kr 30 000 by another, with no utility weights in sight.
    highlight node_cea label_cea effect glow

But the number comes with a fence around it: cost per infection avoided cannot be laid beside cost per hip replaced, so it ranks options only inside its own programme.
    point at.ref label_cea gesture underline

    pause 0.4

Second branch: the options do different kinds of good and reach into the same budget — so they need a common currency, and that currency is the QALY.
    draw edge_q_cua branchlabel_q_cua node_cua label_cua

Nusinersen came in at millions of kroner per QALY; a hip replacement buys one for a small fraction of that — only cost per QALY puts them on the same page for Beslutningsforum.
    highlight node_cua label_cua effect glow

The price of that reach is compression — everything a treatment does squeezed into one utility number — which is exactly why the QALY's fairness stays contested.
    focus node_cua label_cua

    pause 0.3

Third branch: when the benefit leaves health altogether — time saved, a cleaner river — only cost-benefit analysis can hold it, because it prices the benefit in kroner too.
    draw edge_q_cba branchlabel_q_cba node_cba label_cba

And the fourth branch is not really a branch: cost-of-illness compares nothing with nothing.
    draw edge_q_coi branchlabel_q_coi node_coi label_coi

It ranks problems by size, which is why a big burden number travels so well in a press release — but it can never show that spending here buys more health than spending there.
    mark strike_coi target label_coi kind strike

Cost-minimisation fails the same way one level up: it crowns the cheaper option by assuming DeltaE is zero rather than demonstrating it.
    highlight node_q effect pulse

    pause 0.4

So the choice is never about which method is most sophisticated — it is about how wide the comparison must be: one programme, or the whole budget.
    focus node_cea label_cea node_cua label_cua

What must compete with what chooses the method, before a single cost is collected.
    mark box_q target label_q kind box
    delivery grave

Four questions, four answers: natural units when the goal is fixed, QALYs when everything competes for the same krone, kroner when the benefit leaves health — and cost-of-illness, which answers none of them, because it never names an alternative.

